        "summary":  "AWS challenges the industry default of selecting AI models based on per-token pricing, arguing that \u0027cost per successful outcome\u0027 — factoring in accuracy, token efficiency, and agentic turn count — is the correct metric. Benchmarking five models across math, science, and professional deliverable tasks found GPT-5.6-Luna on Amazon Bedrock achieved the lowest observed cost per correct answer despite not having the lowest nominal price. The analysis demonstrates that procurement based on pricing pages alone leads to suboptimal total cost of ownership.",
        "execSummary":  "This article reframes enterprise AI model procurement from a cost-center conversation to an outcome economics conversation, which is the right framing for finance and operations leaders. The finding that the cheapest-per-token model is rarely the cheapest-per-outcome is intuitive but now has benchmark data to back it: a less accurate model that requires more retries or more turns to complete a task quickly erases its headline cost advantage. For customer support and operations deployments specifically, agentic turn count is a first-order cost driver that most vendor comparisons obscure. Procurement teams should demand outcome-based benchmarks on representative workload samples before finalizing model selection.",
