        "title":  "Build your AI stack like you will need to replace it",
        "source":  "InfoWorld",
        "url":  "https://www.infoworld.com/article/4212117/build-your-ai-stack-like-you-will-need-to-replace-it.html",
        "date":  "2026-09-10",
        "theme":  "Platforms",
        "summary":  "The article argues that organizations building AI features should architect systems with vendor portability in mind, as current AI pricing is artificially low and increases are inevitable. Creating an abstraction layer through tools like LiteLLM can help, but deeper dependencies like fine-tuned prompts, vector embeddings, and vendor-specific code conventions create real switching costs. Teams should proactively test their ability to migrate workflows between providers before facing a crisis.",
        "execSummary":  "The central decision this article surfaces for enterprise technology leaders is that AI vendor lock-in risk is underpriced today because model costs are artificially low — and organizations building tightly coupled stacks now will face painful migrations when pricing normalizes. The practical recommendation is to architect for portability from the start using abstraction layers, and to treat AI vendor concentration with the same risk scrutiny applied to cloud or data infrastructure. For enterprise leaders, the key implication is that platform decisions made during the current low-cost period will determine negotiating leverage and operational resilience for years. Teams that proactively test provider migration now — before it is urgent — will be significantly better positioned than those who discover lock-in during a pricing or capability crisis.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
